1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,612
Module 19 is testing and contributing.

2
00:00:01,612 --> 00:00:07,000
Before touching a single line, know how to prove nothing broke — the same two commands the project's own CI runs on every push.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:08,271
npm test runs Vitest over the tests folder.

4
00:00:08,271 --> 00:00:14,803
Hundreds of specs, covering the pure logic across the codebase plus the sharper stuff: the store's streaming path, the tool-call loop, the lazy chat-hydration invariants, the agent loop, the memory store, the voice stack.

5
00:00:14,803 --> 00:00:19,000
This is the same command E04 had you run right after building from source, back in Module 1 — now you're seeing what it's actually protecting.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,322
npx tsc --noEmit is the second half — no test runs, it just type-checks the whole project.

7
00:00:22,322 --> 00:00:27,527
This is what catches a shim whose signature drifted from the real Tauri import, or a component destructuring a store action that got renamed.

8
00:00:27,527 --> 00:00:30,000
Clean output, no errors, is success — silence is the passing state.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,497
Both of these, plus a Vite build and cargo clippy and cargo test on the Rust side, run automatically in ci.

10
00:00:33,497 --> 00:00:34,837
yml on every push and every pull request.

11
00:00:34,837 --> 00:00:40,000
Nothing merges without all four passing — so running them locally first is just doing on your machine what would otherwise fail in public a few minutes later.

12
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,429
Now the Rust side of testing, as a concrete example, revisiting E51.

13
00:00:42,429 --> 00:00:47,286
start_server has a real side effect — it spawns a process — so it can't be unit-tested directly without actually launching llama-server.

14
00:00:47,286 --> 00:00:52,071
The fix: launch_flag_args, the part that turns LaunchOpts into a Vec of command-line strings, was pulled out as its own pure function.

15
00:00:52,071 --> 00:00:54,000
No side effects, so it's trivial to test in isolation.

16
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,736
And the test itself checks the exact compatibility guarantee from E51: a default LaunchOpts, with nothing set, must produce an empty Vec — no flags at all.

17
00:00:58,736 --> 00:01:02,311
That's what lets an older llama-server binary that's never heard of --n-cpu-moe or --fit-target still launch cleanly.

18
00:01:02,311 --> 00:01:05,000
One assertion, no process, no server, and it runs in milliseconds as part of cargo test.

19
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,299
The pattern generalizes past this one file.

20
00:01:06,299 --> 00:01:10,077
Anything with a side effect — spawning a process, hitting the network, touching a real filesystem — resists testing directly.

21
00:01:10,077 --> 00:01:16,000
The fix is almost always the same: pull the decision-making part out as a pure function of its inputs, test that thoroughly, and keep the side effect itself as a thin, un-tested wrapper around it.

22
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:23,000
With the checks understood, next episode is your first real contribution — adding a genuine built-in tool to the codebase, the way a real pull request would, rather than a user-authored script.

